#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079471} :activity/id #uuid "2606d908-5f7c-5e93-840f-c4b49677eeaa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049915 :topic/id #uuid "1e508d95-9539-510f-94a4-75658b0cee48" :topic/math-academy-id 609} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079471 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079636 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves of the names of the sides of a right triangle relative to an angle $\\theta.$ \n\n![](images/4c/4cbe48f70bc4bf67600269883c1260cec2181cf01b50a9675c3177b72d7f954b.png)\n\nThe three fundamental trigonometric ratios, **sine**, **cosine**, and **tangent**, represent the ratios of the sides of a right triangle. The ratios are often shortened to $\\sin$, $\\cos,$ and $\\tan,$ and they are defined as\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin(\\theta) &= \\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{hypotenuse}},  \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\cos(\\theta) &= \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{hypotenuse}},  \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\tan(\\theta) &= \\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{adjacent}}.  \\\n\t}\n$$\n\nFor a given angle $\\theta$, these ratios remain the same no matter what the size of the triangle is. For example, if we were to double the length of each side of a given triangle, the values of these ratios would remain unchanged. \n\nAn easy way of remembering how to compute the different trigonometric ratios is by using the three-syllable acronym **SOH-CAH-TOA**. It stands for:\n\n- SOH $\\rightarrow$ **S**ine = **O**pposite / **H**ypotenuse,\n\n- CAH $\\rightarrow$ **C**osine = **A**djacent / **H**ypotenuse,\n\n- TOA $\\rightarrow$ **T**angent = **O**pposite / **A**djacent." :tutorial/id #uuid "7210f33b-b202-57e9-a4c7-8eb1859c0b97" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2445 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1e8abd38-6d6c-5110-8d68-959bdff3fee0" :step/math-academy-id 454273 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078537 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078249 :question/id #uuid "65c52289-9781-5418-b8c6-fb7693aad674" :question/math-academy-id "e-3240"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eaac3fe1-285b-57ba-956b-66eaf51ded7e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050876}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562469}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Ratios Given a Triangle with Integer Side Lengths"} :step/id #uuid "6fdd25c6-2343-577f-a04a-87a707fb66fe" :step/math-academy-id 454274 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079473}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078538 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078253 :question/id #uuid "c9ac49b1-04df-5cf0-bd38-3bc5b044b612" :question/math-academy-id "e-3245"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c8132c-18c7-5250-a68e-68500bdb926e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048702}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562507}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio Given a Triangle with Non-Integer Side Lengths"} :step/id #uuid "e74f7b83-5514-5f09-862a-b76a91cf0008" :step/math-academy-id 454278 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079474}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078539 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078251 :question/id #uuid "e012dbd0-341b-5f01-a670-539c472471bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3243"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6359c90-3e3e-54f3-a66b-c8f69058a3a3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048144}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562426}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Given a Trigonometric Ratio"} :step/id #uuid "888c8f74-0025-5309-8287-98284c3460d0" :step/math-academy-id 454282 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079475}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079475 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079638 :tutorial/content "Calculators usually have a special key that allows us to compute $\\sin\\theta,$ $\\cos\\theta,$ and $\\tan\\theta$ for some specific value of $\\theta.$\n\nFor example, using a calculator, we can compute the trigonometric ratios for $\\theta = 30^\\circ$ and get the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =0.5 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =0.866... \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =0.577...\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Make sure that the calculator is in degrees mode (instead of radians mode) before you start the computation." :tutorial/id #uuid "a39bc506-d331-5cab-8f8a-576b9841dc2f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2448 :tutorial/title "Calculating Sine, Cosine, and Tangent Using a Calculator"} :step/id #uuid "13831967-c85e-5019-908f-24b534c39b7b" :step/math-academy-id 454285 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079476}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079476 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078540 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078255 :question/id #uuid "81f7e0c1-99bf-5feb-9db2-745595a8b613" :question/math-academy-id "e-3247"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcbd6c22-13de-5a61-90b0-38bd478b4f2f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562455}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Function Using a Calculator"} :step/id #uuid "ce1bf483-f305-5809-bd7a-831475eed974" :step/math-academy-id 454286}] :activity/title "The Trigonometric Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078639}]}
